---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { BASE } from "../../lib/base";
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Canon.app"
  eyebrow="macOS companion"
  current="canon-app"
  description="Canon.app is a planned macOS menu-bar app that will show the local Canon server's status and aliases without the terminal; it is not built yet."
>
  <p class="placeholder-flag"><span class="doc__tbd">Placeholder</span> This page is a stand-in; Canon.app's design and build are a separate, not-yet-started workstream.</p>

  <h2 id="what-it-will-do">What it will do</h2>
  <p>
    Canon.app sits in the macOS menu bar next to the <code>canon service</code> daemon it talks
    to. It will show, at a glance:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>whether the workstation server is running, and on which address (loopback, Tailscale, or stopped);</li>
    <li>the alias list, with one-tap open, copy URL, and copy content ID per alias;</li>
    <li>a restart and a "verify now" action for the current store.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Icon states are planned to mirror the server's own binding rules: a loopback dot, a tailnet
    ring, and a hollow icon when the service is stopped — never a state the CLI itself couldn't
    reach.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-wont-do">What it won't do</h2>
  <p>
    Canon.app only reads and displays what the local <code>canon service</code> already serves. It
    does not publish, grant, or bind on its own — any remote-target action still goes through the
    same explicit <code>--grant</code> gate as the CLI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
  <p>
    Not yet built. Today the CLI's <code>canon status</code> and <code>canon service status</code>
    cover the same ground from a terminal; see the <a href={`${BASE}cli/`}>CLI reference</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .placeholder-flag { display: flex; gap: 0.6rem; align-items: baseline; }
</style>
